{"kind":"request","text":"Fix the intermittent CI failure, and fix the report that hides which test caused it.\n\nThe failing test is `TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects`, cmd/gitseq-mcp/main_test.go:1327. It stands up a resident that never answers, calls whoami, and asserts two things at line 1336: that the call returned `degraded: true`, and that less than three seconds of wall clock elapsed. `orientationTimeout` is two seconds, so the assertion allows one second of slack.\n\nThe invariant it guards held. The observed failure was `elapsed=3.821141541s` with `degraded:true` in the result — the stall was bounded, the timeout fired, and the caller was correctly told the answer was degraded. What failed was only the wall-clock ceiling, and it failed because the reporter runs the whole module: `internal/kernel` takes 207 seconds and `cmd/gs` 193 seconds in the same run, so the machine is saturated and 1.8 seconds of scheduling delay lands on top of a 2-second timeout. Three consecutive runs of `go test -count=1 ./...` reproduced it once; the earlier loop over the reporter failed on two of four attempts. That is a test conflating \"the timeout bounds the wait\", which is the property worth pinning, with \"this machine schedules the goroutine promptly\", which is not.\n\nThe second problem is separable and is why this took so long to find. spike/cmd/gitseq-report/main.go runs `go test -count=1 -json ./...` from the module root, then maps results onto six named cases. This test belongs to no case, so it cannot appear in the table. The overall status goes to fail through the `runErr != nil` branch at main.go:129. The published report therefore reads `Overall: fail` above a table where all six cases pass and every listed test passes, with nothing on the page naming the test that failed. A reader has all-green rows and a red total and no way to connect them. The captured stderr was empty, so even the stderr section added earlier would not have helped: the failure detail is in the JSON stream on stdout, which the reporter parses and then discards for anything outside the case map.","body":{"conditions":"TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects asserts the property it means to guard rather than the machine's scheduling speed — that the wait is bounded by orientationTimeout and the caller is told `degraded`, with any remaining wall-clock ceiling generous enough not to fail under full-module load; the fix does not weaken the assertion that an unanswered resident cannot hang the caller, and the reason for the chosen bound is stated in the test; the suite passes ten consecutive runs of `go test -count=1 ./...` from the module root; gitseq-report names every failing test in its output, including tests that map to no case, so a `fail` status can never be published above an all-passing table with nothing explaining it; the report distinguishes a case failure from a run failure so a reader can tell which occurred; `make test` and `go vet` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}